NNAAMMEE
       pamshadedrelief - generate shaded relief image from an elevation map


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff

       [--ggaammmmaa _g]



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff creates a shaded relief image from an elevation map.  A
       shaded relief image is a visual image of terrain, showing  the  terrain
       as  if  illuminated by oblique light and viewed from above, so that the
       brightess of each spot depends upon its slope.  A common example  of  a
       shaded relief image is a relief map of the Earth, which shows mountains
       and valleys.

       The image ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff creates is as if illumated by a light  source
       from the left.

       The output image is a PAM with tuple type GGRRAAYYSSCCAALLEE.

       The  program  ppaammccrraatteerr is a good thing to use to demonstrate the func‐
       tion of ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff.  It generates a  terrain  map  of  a  cratered
       landscape.

           $ pamcrater | pamshadedrelief | pamx





OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ),  ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff  recog‐
       nizes the following command line option:



       --ggaammmmaa _f_a_c_t_o_r

       --gg _f_a_c_t_o_r
              The  specified  _f_a_c_t_o_r  is used to gamma adjust the image in the
              same manner as performed by ppnnmmggaammmmaa.  The default value is 1.0,
              which  results in a medium contrast image.  Values larger than 1
              lighten the image and reduce contrast, while values less than  1
              darken the image, increasing contrast.

              Note  that  this  is  separate from the gamma correction that is
              part of the definition of the PAM GRAYSCALE format.   The  image
              ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff  generates  is  a  genuine,  gamma-corrected PAM
              GRAYSCALE image in any case.  This  option  simply  changes  the
              contrast  and  may compensate for a display device that does not
              correctly render PAM GRAYSCALE images.




DDEESSIIGGNN NNOOTTEESS
       The--ggaammmmaa option isn't really necessary since you can achieve the  same
       effect  by  piping  the  output  from ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff through ppnnmmggaammmmaa.
       However, ppaammsshhaaddeeddrreelliieeff performs an internal gamma map anyway  in  the
       process of rendering the elevation array into the PAM GRAYSCALE format,
       so there's no additional overhead in allowing an additional  gamma  ad‐
       justment.



